"## Selected phenomenon: The origin of powered flight in birds\n\n**Phenomenon:** The evolution of powered, flapping flight in birds — i.e., how the lineage leading to modern birds (starting from small feathered maniraptoran theropods like *Archaeopteryx*) acquired the ability to fly.\n\n**The two candidate explanations I will compare:**\n\n1. **The arboreal (\"trees-down\") hypothesis** — Flight evolved in tree-dwelling ancestors that first glided between trees and branches, with flapping flight developing later from gliding. Gravity assists the transition, making it the energetically easier route. Modern analogues include flying squirrels, sugar gliders, and *Draco* lizards. First proposed by O.C. Marsh in the late 1870s; supported today by evidence for gliding in four-winged *Microraptor* and the arboreal, membrane-winged scansoriopterygids (*Yi qi*, *Epidexipteryx*).\n\n2. **The cursorial (\"ground-up\") hypothesis** — Flight evolved in fast-running, ground-dwelling bipeds, with proto-wings initially serving non-aerodynamic functions (insect netting, prey pouncing, display) and gradually acquiring lift-producing ones. Proposed by Williston (1879), elaborated by Nopcsa, revived by Ostrom in the 1970s, and modernized by Ken Dial's 2003 wing-assisted incline running (WAIR) experiments, which show even rudimentary wings aid running birds.\n\n**Why this phenomenon qualifies:** English Wikipedia treats this as a genuinely open, two-sided debate. The articles \"Origin of birds,\" \"Bird flight,\" \"Archaeopteryx,\" \"Microraptor,\" \"Scansoriopterygidae,\" and \"Wing-assisted incline running\" each present both scenarios with supporting evidence and specific criticisms — neither is ruled out, and Wikipedia notes the dichotomy is increasingly blurred rather than resolved (WAIR blends ground running with tree access; gliding models blur into flapping). Each explanation has testable strengths and weaknesses: trees-down struggles with the lack of unambiguous perching adaptations in basal paravians and the arid, low-vegetation Solnhofen habitat; ground-up struggles with the energetic cost of working against gravity and the utility of small proto-wings at low speeds.\n\n**Runners-up considered and set aside:**\n- **K–Pg extinction (Chicxulub impact vs. Deccan Traps volcanism):** Both are discussed on Wikipedia, but the article presents impact as the strong consensus primary cause, making the comparison lopsided.\n- **Origin of the Moon (giant impact vs. fission/capture/co-accretion):** Wikipedia presents the three classical alternatives as largely ruled out on compositional and dynamical grounds.\n- **Abiogenesis (RNA world vs. metabolism-first):** Plausible and unresolved, but the debate is multi-axial (location × mechanism × genetics-first vs. metabolism-first), less cleanly a two-way comparison.\n\nThe origin of bird flight therefore offers the best combination of genuine balance, rich fossil and biomechanical evidence, and thorough treatment across multiple English Wikipedia articles."